kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-4.logic.r1 and kant-practical-4.logic.r1.2 have "¬love_in_law"; kant-practical-4.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the maxim of doing good from love and our own good pleasure sets the determining principle of our will in the law itself and in respect for this law" is true, which the second allows
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r1 and kant-practical-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the maxim of doing good from love and our own good pleasure sets the determining principle of our will in the law itself and in respect for this law" is true. Is S1, "It is a very beautiful thing to do good to men from love to them and from sympathetic good will, or to be just from love of order; but this is not yet the true moral maxim of our conduct which is suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men, when we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, like volunteers, and, as if we were independent on the command, to want to do of our own good pleasure what we think we need no command to do.", true then?
kant-practical-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: kant-practical-4.logic.r1 and kant-practical-4.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; kant-practical-4.logic.r1.3 has "¬love_in_law"; they differ when only "the maxim of doing good from love and our own good pleasure sets the determining principle of our will in the law itself and in respect for this law" is true, which the first allows
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/negation: kant-practical-4.logic.r1 P1 (S2): the sentence says "nor", but P1 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "we stand under a discipline of reason"
